[signal arg="bg:schwert_mountain"]
Six-year-old Lan Schwert sat cross-legged on the woodpile in the gray mountain light, watching his father split logs, and waited for his moment.
Lan (Talk): Papa.
Narr: Mm.
Lan (Talk): Old Hess down the valley said his chest hurts when he gets angry. He said it's his gi. It goes up to here.
Lan (Talk): But I thought gi went down first. In your stomach.
The axe stopped mid-swing.
Narr: Where'd you hear that?
Lan (Acting): Hess[pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed]
Narr: There's no Hess. Fourth one this month, kid. The porridge one was good. The one where you pretended to fall off the fence was excellent.
Narr: Figure it out yourself.
Lan (Sus): (It isn't that my family is stupid. Mama knows every plant on the mountain.)
Lan (Sus): (Auntie put a hand on the kitchen table once and the whole house went thickly quiet.)
Lan (Sus): (Grandpa sits on the porch all day and birds never land in the yard.)
Lan (Sus): (They teach me everything. The moment anything gets close to gi, or shir, or the sword, a door shuts behind their eyes. Cheerfully. Which is worse.)
Lan (Sus): (I know this game. Two thousand four hundred and fifty hours. I do not know the name Schwert. Not a quest, not a credit, not one line.)
Lan (Default): (So it's background. A random mountain family in a corner the camera never pointed at.)
label trader
One autumn a trader came up the mountain road, saw Narr chopping wood, and went pale as old paper.
He turned his cart around on a track barely wide enough for it and went straight back down.
Lan (Talk): Papa, who was that man?
Narr: Him? Ah. He'd have known me back when people still called me the Fool.
Lan (Talk): The Fool?
Narr: Mm. Eat your carrots.
Across the table, his mother didn't look up from her bowl, and she didn't smile. She smiled at everything.
label exile
On his tenth birthday, without ceremony, the Law of Exile arrived.
Out of the valley, and no coming back until you'd made a name the whole continent could hear.
His mother packed four days of food. His father handed him a wooden sword, heavy and plain.
Lan (Talk): This is a stick.
Narr: It's a good stick.
Narr: Go on then. Make a name for yourself. A big one. And don't come back until you've found a beautiful wife, you hear me?
Lan walked down the mountain road with his eyes stinging and no idea why.
From the first bend, his grandfather was still on the porch, and he was fairly sure the old man was crying.
label forest
[signal arg="bg:border_forest"]
Six years taught him what the mountain never did. The wooden sword lasted nine months.
He replaced it with a steel one taken off a bandit who no longer needed it.
Lan (Sus): (My gi gathers. It will not stay.)
Lan (Sus): (I'm leaking more than half of everything I have, and there's no guide for it, because in the game you just pressed a button.)
His swordsmanship had become something that made grown mercenaries go quiet.
Around fourteen he spent nearly everything he owned on a montante,
a huge two-handed sword with a double ring guard, because old Iberian styles were what his hands remembered best.
Lan (Sus): (One teacher. That's all I need. And the only door to that is Tausend Sterne Academy.)
Lan (Sus): (No papers, a country accent, and eleven silver coins.)
Four days later, in the middle of an ordinary escort job, the forest went wrong.
A guard screamed. Hold the line! The front's broken, the front's[pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed]
A Horror Boar came through the barricade the way a storm comes through a window. The escort was gone in under a minute.
Lan came out of it alone, on his feet, the montante bare in his hands.
Lan (Combat): (Fine.)
[signal arg="combat:boar"]
label after_boar
Then his grip failed. His fingers opened on their own, and for one half-second he was back in an apartment, listening to a sword ring off the floor.
Lan (Mad): (Not this again.)
Berthram had been watching for three minutes and forty seconds. He had walked up the ridge planning to eat his lunch. He did not go down right away.
Berthram: Ah. Are you all right, young man?
An old man walked out of the tree line holding a map upside down.
Silver beard, a scar across the left eye, an apple in his hand. He raised his free hand.
There was no chant. A column of pure sun-colored energy simply appeared. Then there was only ash, a smoking hole, and the smell of struck metal.
Lan (Sus): (That's Berthram. The Empire Sun. In the game you had to survive to year four to see this man fight.)
Berthram: Hm. You're throwing away more than half your output. It's going straight out through your wrist, like a leak.
Berthram: Nobody's ever taught you to seal that, have they.
Lan (Mad): No one's ever taught me anything.
Berthram: Four minutes.
Lan (Talk): What?
Berthram: Nothing. Your name, boy.
Lan (Default): Lan. Lan Schwert.
And the old man's face went out like a snuffed candle. The smile didn't fall. It froze, every line in place, while something behind it left the room.
Lan (Sus): (He knows that name. Nobody knows that name. I don't even know that name.)
Berthram: Schwert, you say. Well, well. A rough diamond, rusting in the mud. How would you like to sit the entrance trials at Tausend Sterne?
Lan (Sus): (Would you like to unlock and reach the TRUE ENDING?)
Lan (Default): I accept.
Berthram: Good lad. Trials are next spring, I'll send the letter. One condition.
Berthram: In your first month you'll come to my office and answer some questions about your family. Honestly. All of them.
Berthram: And if it turns out you can't answer them, then you and I are going to have a very long talk.
label trials
[signal arg="bg:trials_hall"]
He spent a year preparing.
Eighty-four spots, and every applicant ranked one through eighty-four, the number stuck to your name for the whole first year.
Lan (Sus): (Don't get noticed. Don't get recruited. Don't get watched.)
Lan (Sus): (He enrolls this year, in this department. Same classes, same ground.)
Lan (Sus): (Close enough to steer him away from anything that would kill him, far enough back to leave no mark.)
The practical test. A plain steel practice sword. The montante stayed wrapped.
- Keep it dull
	Lan (Sus): (Dull. Forgettable. A farm boy with textbook footwork.)
- Look bored
	Lan (Smug): (If I'm going to be forgettable, I might as well commit to the bit.)
set {rank} = 67
A clean, unhurried show of basic technique. Solid footwork, nothing that would make anyone lean forward in their seat.
He let two exchanges go that he could have won.
When they handed him the form to choose his department, he didn't hesitate. PHYSICAL DEPARTMENT.
label letter
The letter came three weeks later on heavy cream paper with the academy's seal in blue wax.
SCHWERT, LAN. Physical Department. First Year. Entrance Rank {rank} of 84.
He read it twice, and that's when he noticed the second page. A registration slip.
In the box marked FAMILY / HOUSE, a clerk had written Schwert.
Next to it, in a different pen, a small red mark and three words.
Verify with Headmaster.
Lan (Sus): (Two thousand four hundred and fifty hours, and the very first thing this world hands me is a file I've never read.)
